now from potential future conservative  MPs to one of the party's most senior  figures the former leader in Duggan  Smith joins us now thank you very much  for being with us on the ground today  now  just speaking to Nigel frosh he says  he's open to a pact with the  Conservative Party at the next election  yesterday Jeremy Corbyn was in your seat  announcing his plans to try and scrap  universal credit he clearly thinks he  can win in that seat would a pathway the  brexit Party help you know I'm not  interested in really what other parties  say or do  Jeremy Corbyn was distinctly unwelcome  in my constituency and about 70 people  turned up and he shipped them all in  I've been doing nothing but campaigning  in my seat since the 2017 election and  what I do see is a lot of labour  supporters that used to support labour  now moving away from labour because of  the kind of appalling extremism  anti-semitism of the labour party so I'm  not in the slightest bit troubled by  what he was doing there I hope you  witnesses that for himself the reality  for us as a party and I heard you  earlier questioning is to make sure that  we recognize there's a distinct  difference between being supposedly in  the centre ground and then also  delivering on what democratically the  majority of the public have actually  voted for it's not an extreme position  to want to deliver brexit for those who  voted when they were promised or  whatever they succeeded in voting for  would be delivered and then there's all  the domestic stuff and I'm absolutely  the moon this morning because I have  campaigned for 20 years to get extra  money brought into the hospital my local  hospitals to have a rebuild and finally  today that 400 million has been  announced which will is a huge Philip to  all the doctors and nurses and people  who work in which Cross Hospital and I'm  over the moon that we finally got a  Conservative government not a Labour  government a Conservative government  investing in the future of whip across  hospital I think that's fantastic you're  talking about the need to deliver Brack  said to deliver the result of the  referendum and we've been speaking on  the show quite a lot about the been at  this idea that Parliament's compelled  the Prime Minister to seek an extension  rather than leave without a deal do you  think that the Prime Minister should  nor Parliament if a deal can't be  secured in time well it's not really  about ignoring Parliament the question  is really what did Parliament vote for  first of all this suppose it's surrender  Act which people refer to sometimes as  the been act is a mess it's a complete  shambles it had an amendment made to it  we don't know for certain whether that  amendment makes it absolute that they  have to write on the basis of the  amendment or on the basis of the  original text there's also the other  question which is really why does a  European Union administration why would  they want to force some kind of  extension on a clearly unwilling  government that does not want to have  that extension so there's an awful lot  of debate and discussion to be had but  Boris Johnson's quite within his rights  absolutely to say a personal to himself  he will not sign such an act I frankly  wouldn't do it myself either and we've  gone through a period where really  there's a lot of pompous and nonsense  being taught by people about  parliamentary sovereignty the people are  sovereign and they give their sovereign  authority to Parliament who exercise it  but when they have made a decision like  they've made and then they've got MPs  and all parties conniving to stop them  that's hardly the centre ground that is  the extreme and I think of those who are  blocking brexit now as extremists not  sent to ground people the centre ground  of British politics is to deliver on  what people voted for and that is what  Boris Johnson's determined to do and no  surrender Act should stop us I mean  you're talking about there that it's not  the centre ground to you know try and  stop brexit from happening but I think I  guess what some of the people expelled  from the party would argue is that  people have different views on brexit  that shouldn't mean that they can't be  in the broad tent of the Conservative  Party Philip Hammond for example telling  the times yesterday that the party is  unrecognizable to him in its place is an  ideological Puritanism that Brooks no  dissent I mean that's what's happening  is it no it's pompous claptrap honestly  all political parties you know I've been  in 27 years and I've rebelled against my  own government but there are times when  the party says to you categorically if  there are specific issues around how you  govern and how  you take that forward this is a vote of  confidence if you choose when it's clear  for reasonable reasons and after all as  one of your earlier guests said the  reason this was a vote of confidence  because Parliament was seeking to take  the power from government to actually  legislate which is almost completely  unprecedented therefore it's quite right  that a government says it's a vote of  confidence in the government if you vote  to strip power from that government now  people like the ex Chancellor and others  Oliver let when and kono very well that  had they been in any other time they  would have absolutely opposed such a  motion and so the very fact is that they  chose to do that was actually to vote  against their party's very strong wishes  and so they knew what they were doing  now I can remain friends with those  individuals I have much time for many of  them but I simply say to them you now  need to think about how you support your  government because that is the way back  I want to see as many as possible come  back into the Conservative Party but  they must start supporting the party and  the government it is not an extreme  government to make stupid statements  like that is to play holi into the hands  of 